# Line-ending policy.
#
# The platform is developed on Windows and runs on Linux. Git's autocrlf will happily hand a shell
# script CRLF endings in the working tree, and that file is then bind-mounted straight into a
# container — where bash reads `set -euo pipefail\r` and reports:
#
#     /scripts/create-topics.sh: line 16: set: pipefail: invalid option name
#
# It then carries on with `set -e` inactive and fails silently partway through. That is exactly how
# the M11 acceptance run got 27 of its 43 Kafka topics and no error.
#
# Anything executed by a Linux shell, or read by a Linux tool, is therefore pinned to LF regardless
# of platform or of a developer's core.autocrlf setting.

* text=auto

*.sh          text eol=lf
*.bash        text eol=lf
Dockerfile    text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile  text eol=lf
.dockerignore text eol=lf
*.yml         text eol=lf
*.yaml        text eol=lf
*.sql         text eol=lf
*.proto       text eol=lf

# Windows-only helpers keep CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binary — never touch.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.jar  binary
